  "seven_q": {
    "Q0_posture": "Analytical and rigorous; establishes a structural isomorphism between physics and economics with explicit mathematical form, testable predictions, and falsification criteria.",
    "Q1_identity": "An isomorphism record (ISO-037) documenting a structural mapping between the Beer-Lambert law of absorption in optics and transaction fee extraction in economics/theology.",
    "Q2_domain": "Physics (optics), Economics (payment infrastructure), Theology (false balance/coherence degradation), Mathematics (exponential decay).",
    "Q3_claim": "Transaction fee extraction follows the exact mathematical form of Beer-Lambert absorption: P(n) = P\u2080(1-f)^n \u2248 P\u2080e^(-fn), where the fee rate f is the absorption coefficient and the number of transactions n is the path length. This is a structural isomorphism, not merely an analogy.",
    "Q4_support": "Mathematical derivation showing identity of form; element-by-element mapping table; swap test passed; quantitative examples ($20 through 10/30 transactions); four explicit falsification criteria; citation of Beer-Lambert history and economic data sources.",
    "Q5_dependencies": "Beer-Lambert law as established physics; existence of transaction fee infrastructure; mandatory passage through digital payment systems; conservation laws (BC7); A1.1 (Existence).",
    "Q6_consequences": "Predicts exponential decay of purchasing power with digital payment penetration; intermediary layer captures increasing share of GDP; disproportionate impact on poor (high-frequency, low-value transactions); cash elimination increases effective \u03b1 of economy.",
    "Q7_kill": "Show transaction fee extraction does NOT follow exponential decay; show economies with mandatory digital payment do NOT have higher intermediary extraction; show buyers control interchange rates; show increasing digital payment does NOT correlate with increasing intermediary GDP share."
  },
